        "text": "Over the years, Richard Wiseman has tried to uncover the truth about deception — investigating the signs that give a liar away.\n\nA  In the 1970s, as part of a large research programme exploring communication between species, Dr Francine Patterson of Stanford University tried to teach two lowland gorillas called Michael and Koko a simplified version of sign language. According to Patterson, the great apes were capable of holding meaningful conversations, and could even think about deep subjects such as love and death. During the project, their trainers believe they found cases where the two gorillas' language skills seemed to provide reliable evidence of deliberate deceit. In one example, Koko broke a toy cat, and then signed to show that one of her trainers had caused the damage. In another, Michael tore a jacket that belonged to a trainer and, when asked who was responsible, signed 'Koko'. When the trainer expressed some doubt, Michael appeared to change his mind and indicated that Dr Patterson was actually responsible, before finally confessing.\n\nB  Other researchers have explored how deception develops in children. Some of the most interesting experiments have involved asking young children not to peek at their favourite toys. In these studies, a child is led into a laboratory and asked to face one of the walls. The experimenter then explains that he is going to set up an elaborate toy a few feet behind them. After setting up the toy, the experimenter says that he has to leave the laboratory, and asks the child not to turn around and peek at the toy. The child is secretly filmed by hidden cameras for a few minutes, and then the experimenter returns and asks whether they peeked. Almost all three-year-olds do, and then half of them lie about it to the experimenter. By the time the children have reached the age of five, all of them peek and all of them lie. The results provide strong evidence that lying starts to appear the moment we learn to speak.\n\nC  So what are the tell-tale signs that give away a lie? In 1994, the psychologist Richard Wiseman designed a large-scale experiment on a TV programme called Tomorrow's World. As part of the experiment, viewers watched two interviews in which Wiseman asked a presenter in front of the cameras to describe his favourite film. In one interview, the presenter chose Some Like It Hot and told the truth; in the other, he chose Gone with the Wind and lied. Viewers were then invited to phone in and say which film he was lying about. More than 30,000 calls were received, but viewers could not tell the difference and the vote was split 50/50. In similar experiments, the results have been remarkably consistent: when it comes to detecting lies, people might as well just toss a coin. It does not matter whether you are male or female, young or old; very few people are able to detect deception.\n\nD  Why is this? Professor Charles Bond of Texas Christian University has carried out surveys into the kinds of behaviour people associate with lying. He has interviewed thousands of people from more than 60 countries, asking them to describe how they decide whether someone is lying. People's answers are remarkably consistent. Almost everyone thinks liars tend to look away, wave their hands around nervously and shift about in their seats. There is, however, one small problem. Researchers have spent hour after hour carefully comparing films of liars and of people telling the truth. The results are clear. Liars do not necessarily look away from you; they do not appear nervous and move their hands around or shift about in their seats. People fail to detect lies because they are basing their opinions on behaviours that are not actually associated with deception.\n\nE  So what are we missing? Clearly, the more information you give away, the greater the chance that some of it will come back to cause you trouble. As a result, liars tend to say less and give fewer details than people who tell the truth. Looking back at the transcripts of the interviews with the presenter, his lie about Gone with the Wind contained about 40 words, while the truth about Some Like It Hot was nearly twice as long. People who lie also try psychologically to keep a distance from their lies, and so they tend to refer to themselves less in their stories. In his whole interview about Gone with the Wind, the presenter mentioned only once how the film made him feel, compared with several references to his feelings when he talked about Some Like It Hot.\n\nF  The simple fact is that the real clues to deceit are in the words people use, not in their body language. So do people become better lie detectors when they listen to a liar, or even just read a transcript of what they said? The interviews with the presenter were also broadcast on radio and published in a newspaper, and although the television viewers were no better than chance at detecting the lie, the newspaper readers were right 64% of the time, and the radio listeners scored an impressive 73%."
